    meditation:
      "Paul writes that the Gentiles, who never saw Sinai, still bear the law within them — their conscience accusing, or defending, between their own thoughts. The Latin word he uses, conscientia, means literally the knowing-with: a witness inside you who knows what you know, who saw what you saw, who heard the room go quiet when the senior partner said \"draft it the other way.\"\n\nThis is the first thing to say about conscience for a lawyer. It is not a feeling. It is a witness. And the advocate, of all people, knows what a witness is for.\n\nMore is in Antwerp in the dialogue of Utopia, talking with the traveler Raphael Hythloday about whether a thoughtful man should serve at court. Hythloday says no — counsel given to a prince who has already made up his mind is wasted breath, and worse, becomes cover. More answers with his \"other philosophy, more civil\" — the one that knows the stage it walks onto, that plays its part \"with comeliness,\" speaking nothing out of due order. He is sketching the case for staying in. For taking the brief. For doing the indirect, possible good rather than the direct, impossible one.\n\nIt is a beautiful passage and a dangerous one, because More himself eventually discovers its limit. The civil philosophy works until the prince asks for the oath. Then the stage collapses and there is only the witness inside, the one who knows.\n\nYou will recognize the situation. You are not Lord Chancellor; you are six years in, on the eighth floor, drafting a response to interrogatory number twelve. The senior associate has marked up your draft. Where you wrote \"the Company is aware of three prior incidents,\" she has written \"the Company is not presently aware of incidents matching the definition set forth in Interrogatory 12.\" It is technically correct under the negotiated definition. It is, you know, substantively misleading. The plaintiff's lawyer will read it and conclude there are no incidents. There are three.\n\nYou can argue with the markup. You can let it through. You can ask for a meeting. You can resign. Most of those options will cost you something. One of them — the easiest one — costs you only the witness.\n\nThe Greek word Paul uses, syneidesis, is built the same way as the Latin. Syn, with. Eidesis, knowing. The faculty in you that knows along with you what you have done. It is not, in the ancient understanding, a separate organ; it is your own mind doubled back on itself, watching what it is up to. The advocate, who spends his working hours watching what other people are up to and giving testimony about it in writing, has unusual training for this interior task. He already knows how to read a transcript carefully. He already knows what it sounds like when a witness is being shaded. He can, if he is honest, apply the same skills to his own conduct.\n\nWhat the tradition teaches, and what More learned the hard way, is that the witness is the one thing you cannot pay. He cannot be reassigned. He cannot be billed to the matter. He follows you out of the office and into the car and into the bedroom and into the parish on Sunday and into the long Saturday morning of your sixty-eighth year when you sit down to think about your life. He is patient. He is not in a hurry. He has time.\n\nNotice what Paul does not say. He does not say conscience is infallible. He says it bears witness — sometimes accusing, sometimes defending — and that the witness is between your own thoughts. It is internal litigation. The same faculty that condemns you when you have lied also clears you when you have been falsely accused. It is not the law itself; it is the courtroom in which you stand to be heard.\n\nMore's \"civil philosophy\" is not wrong. There is a real virtue in staying at the table, in shaping the possible from inside the room rather than denouncing from the road. Most days, that is the work. Most days, the markup is fine, the warranty is honest, the press release is accurate, and you go home and sleep. The advocate who refuses every compromise will be the advocate of no one, and the unrepresented will lose by default while he polishes his integrity.\n\nBut the civil philosophy has a floor. Below a certain point, the comely playing of your part becomes the prince's cover. You are not advising; you are absolving. You are not counseling; you are giving him what he wanted to hear with a colorable footnote attached. And the witness inside will know. He always knows.\n\nThe rest of this week is about him. About the witness. About what it costs to keep him honest, and what it costs more to silence him. We will sit with More in the Tower and with Newman in his study and with Aquinas in the schools. We will look at the cases where conscience errs and what we owe it even then. We will read More's last letter to his daughter, written from a stone room with a piece of coal, when there was no more stage to play upon and only the witness was left.\n\nFor today, simply notice that you have one. Notice the small interior pause when the markup changes the meaning. Notice the second sentence you almost said in the deposition and then did not. Notice the line in the press release that made you look at the ceiling. That is him, the knowing-with, the one Paul says God put in the Gentile heart so that no one would ever be without a court.\n\nYou are an advocate. You know what a witness is for. Treat your own with the dignity you would demand for any other.",
