    weekArc: "Conscience",
    weekArcLatin: "Conscientia",
    title: "The Young Associate's First Lie",
    scripture: {
      reference: "Luke 16:10",
      text: "He that is faithful in that which is least, is faithful also in that which is greater: and he that is unjust in that which is little, is unjust also in that which is greater.",
    },
    excerpt: {
      author: "Augustine of Hippo",
      citation: "Confessions, Book II.4 (Pusey trans., 1838)",
      text: "Theft is punished by Thy law, O Lord, and the law written in the hearts of men, which iniquity itself effaces not. For what thief will abide a thief? not even a rich thief, one stealing through want. Yet I lusted to thieve, and did it, compelled by no hunger, nor poverty, but through a cloyedness of well-doing, and a pamperedness of iniquity. For I stole that, of which I had enough, and much better. Nor cared I to enjoy what I stole, but joyed in the theft and sin itself. A pear tree there was near our vineyard, laden with fruit, tempting neither for colour nor taste. To shake and rob this, some lewd young fellows of us went, late one night.",
    },
    meditation:
      "The first lie is almost never important. That is its whole genius.\n\nA partner asks if you have reviewed the second amendment to the side letter. You have skimmed it. You say yes. The matter does not turn on the side letter. The partner does not ask a follow-up. The conversation moves on. You feel, for a half-second, the small interior cost — and then a meeting starts and you forget.\n\nAugustine in the second book of the Confessions writes about stealing pears. He goes out of his way to insist that the pears were not even good. He had better fruit at home. He was not hungry. The theft was not the kind of thing a future bishop would later need to apologize for in any serious way; it was a boys' prank in a Carthaginian summer. And yet he devotes one of the most famous passages in Western literature to it, because he saw, in retrospect, that this small unimportant act was the shape of every later sin. He had loved doing the wrong thing because it was wrong. The act was empty; the formation was complete.\n\nOur Lord's line in Luke is the same point in the other direction. Fidelity in the small thing is fidelity in the great thing, because there is in the end only one fidelity, expressed in many sizes. The young associate's first lie is small. The partner's career-ending lie, twenty years later, is large. They are the same lie. The intervening years are the long tutorial in which the small one taught the lawyer how to tell the large one without flinching.\n\nThis is unfashionable to say. The dominant vocabulary in legal ethics is risk. Don't lie because you might get caught. Don't lie because the bar will discipline you. Don't lie because the documents are discoverable. These are real considerations and you should take them seriously. But they are the wrong reason, and a lawyer who is honest only because of risk is honest only when the risk is visible. The interesting cases — the ones that actually corrode a practice — are the ones with no risk. The casual exaggeration on the phone with opposing counsel. The slightly inflated description of the case to the client. The thing you said to your spouse about how the day went.\n\nThe Christian tradition is unembarrassed about this. It says you are being formed. Every act, however small, leaves a deposit. The fourth time you say \"I'll have that to you by end of day\" knowing you will not, you have not just made a fourth small misrepresentation. You have practiced, four times, the muscle of saying a comforting falsehood to make a present moment easier. The muscle gets stronger. The fifth time is easier. By the fortieth time, it is so natural you do not even notice you have done it, and that is the point at which you are dangerous.\n\nThe pear tree is not the bar discipline. The pear tree is the formation. Augustine saw that the unimportant theft had taught him a posture: that pleasure could be taken in evading, in getting away with, in the small interior thrill of having done the forbidden thing without consequence. That posture, repeated, becomes a person. The bishop he later became had to spend years unlearning it.\n\nThe lawyer's pear trees are easy to list. The hour you billed that you didn't quite work — you were thinking about the matter, sort of, on the drive home. The CLE you claimed credit for but napped through. The expense report rounded up. The reference you wrote for a former colleague who was, in truth, mediocre. The compliment to the client who asked for your honest opinion on his terrible idea. The promise to a pro bono client that you would call this week, when you knew you wouldn't.\n\nNone of these is, by itself, a matter for the disciplinary committee. All of them, together, are how you became the lawyer who, fifteen years later, found himself able to sign a misleading certification without losing sleep. The signature did not come out of nowhere. It came out of forty thousand small fidelities that had quietly drifted into forty thousand small infidelities, until the difference between the two had been worn smooth.\n\nThe inverse is also true, which is the gospel news inside the warning. Faithful in little, faithful in much. The associate who, in the small unwatched moments, tells the truth when a lie would have been easier and cheaper is forming the lawyer who, twenty years later, will be the one in the room when the certification is asked for, and the one who can say, plainly and without drama, \"we cannot sign that.\" He will be able to say it because he has practiced. Not by rehearsing the big moments, which never come the way you rehearsed them, but by being faithful in the side-letter conversation, the time entry, the expense report, the reference, the email about the deadline.\n\nThis is why the moral life of a lawyer is finally not heroic. The heroes — More on the scaffold, Becket at the altar — got there by a thousand small days of integrity that no one noticed and that left no record. They were the ones who, on a Tuesday afternoon at the office in March, told the small unimportant truth when the small unimportant lie would have done. The scaffold was the natural ending of the Tuesday afternoons.\n\nToday's pear tree is whatever you almost say without thinking. Notice it. Tell the small truth instead. Bill the actual hour. Admit you have not read the side letter. Tell the client you missed his call because you forgot, not because you were on another call. The pears are not the point. The orchard you become is.",
    prayer:
      "Lord, I am being formed today whether I notice or not. In the small matters no one is watching, let me be the kind of man who tells the truth out of habit. Forgive the easy untruths I have practiced into competence. Make me faithful in the little thing, that I might be faithful when the great thing comes.",
    prompt:
      "What is the smallest, most habitual untruth in your week? What would it cost to stop telling it?",
