    meditation:
      "Isaias is talking about legislators. He is also, by extension, talking about anyone who drafts. The prophet's woe falls on those who write the rule that becomes the instrument of oppression — and a great deal of modern oppression is written, in eleven-point type, by lawyers, on letterhead.\n\nThe rules of professional conduct in your jurisdiction are mostly good. They were written by serious people, refined over a century, and they articulate something close to a working morality for the profession. Most days, if you follow them, you are doing the right thing. A young lawyer should know them cold, follow them carefully, and treat the disciplinary process with respect.\n\nBut the rules are not your conscience. They are the bar's. The two overlap most of the time and diverge in a few places that matter very much.\n\nAquinas's question in the Summa is whether human law binds in conscience. His answer is layered. Just laws bind, because they derive from the eternal law. Unjust laws — those contrary to the common good, or beyond the lawmaker's authority, or unequally distributed — do not bind in conscience, though one may still obey them to avoid scandal or worse harm. He is not a libertarian; he is not in love with disobedience. He is making the precise point that law derives its binding force from its participation in justice. Where it ceases to participate, the binding loosens.\n\nApply this to the rules of professional conduct. Most of them participate in justice. The duty of competence, the duty to avoid conflicts, the duty to keep client funds in trust — these are not arbitrary; they protect real people from real harms, and a lawyer who violates them has done something wrong both under the rule and in conscience.\n\nBut some rules, in some applications, do not. Consider the duty of zealous advocacy as it is sometimes interpreted in the discovery context. The rule does not require you to play the document-production game to its grim conclusion — to bury the responsive material in the irresponsive, to construe every request as narrowly as language permits, to assert privilege on documents that are not really privileged because the other side will not have the resources to fight it. The rule does not require any of that. But the bar's enforcement of the rule rarely sanctions it, and the culture of large-firm litigation has built an entire practice on the edges of it. You can do all of these things and not be disciplined. You can do all of these things and still have, in the strict sense, violated nothing.\n\nAnd yet a lawyer with a working conscience knows that some of these maneuvers are wrong. They are wrong not because the bar says so but because they obstruct the actual finding of facts in a dispute that involves real human beings whose lives turn on the result. The widow in Isaias is the plaintiff who could not afford the second round of motions and went away. The fatherless are the clients whose case settled for ten cents on the dollar because their lawyer's small firm could not keep up with the document production. The prophet's woe is not narrowly aimed at the legislature. It falls on the whole architecture of writing that turns law into a weapon against the weak.\n\nThis is where the bar's ethics and the lawyer's conscience part ways, and you have to be honest about which one you are following at any given moment. The discovery-response that's technically truthful and substantively misleading is permitted, sometimes encouraged, often rewarded. You can pass the disciplinary screen with flying colors and have done a serious wrong. The deposition coaching that stays just on the right side of the rule — \"answer only the question asked, don't volunteer\" repeated until the witness no longer remembers what he saw — is permitted. The takeover defense that uses every procedural lever to wear the bidder down — permitted. The press release written so that every sentence is literally true and the whole document conveys a false impression — permitted.\n\nThe rules cannot do the whole work of an ethical life. They were never designed to. Aquinas knew that human law is necessarily general and that the application to particular cases requires the virtue of prudence, which lives in the individual judgment of the actor. The rule says do not lie. Prudence — informed by the witness inside — has to decide whether the press release is a lie. That decision the bar cannot make for you. It can only set the outer perimeter beyond which it will punish. Most of the moral life of a lawyer happens inside that perimeter, and the perimeter is a great deal larger than the field you should actually be playing in.\n\nThe inverse case is rarer but real. Sometimes a rule, applied to your situation, would require you to do something your conscience forbids. The mandatory-reporting rule that would betray a confidence which protects an innocent party. The withdrawal rule that, applied mechanically, would leave a client unrepresented at a critical moment. The candor-to-the-tribunal rule in tension with the confidentiality rule on a fact that emerged after a brief was filed. In these cases the lawyer has work to do — to read the rule carefully, to seek out the commentary, to consult a wise colleague, to find the path that satisfies both rule and conscience if one exists, and to be ready to take the discipline if it does not.\n\nThe practical posture, in the end, is this. Know the rules. Follow them. Treat the disciplinary process with respect. And then, having done all that, do not mistake the rules for the moral law. They are the floor, not the ceiling. The widow at the end of the deposition does not care that you complied with Rule 30. She cares whether you treated her like a person. The witness inside you will know the difference.",
    prayer:
      "Lord, you wrote your law in my heart before any bar wrote its rules. Let me follow the rules of my profession with diligence and the law of conscience with greater diligence still. Where they agree, give me obedience. Where they part, give me prudence and the courage to follow the higher of the two.",
    prompt:
      "Where in your current practice does the bar's permission exceed your conscience's? What are you doing about it?",
