    excerpt: {
      author: "Thomas More",
      citation: "Letter to Margaret Roper, from the Tower of London, 1534 (Rogers ed., 1947)",
      text: "Surely, Megge, a fainter heart than thy frail father hath, canst thou not have. And yet I verily trust in the great mercy of God, that He shall of His goodness so stay me with His holy hand that He shall not finally suffer me to fall wretchedly from His favour. And the like trust, dear daughter, in His high goodness I verily conceive of you. And so much the more, in that you take, for the love of Him, so godly the loss of your worldly substance, lands, livelihood, and being driven to live in a strange country to His high pleasure. Be of good cheer, my good daughter, and take no thought for me, though I look up sadly, for I have a good hope that God shall make me a strong champion in His own quarrel.",
    },
    meditation:
      "Paul writes from prison, near the end. The metaphors are athletic and martial: the good fight fought, the course finished, the faith kept. The verbs are in the perfect tense. The work is done. There is a crown laid up. He is not boasting; he is reporting. He has been faithful and he knows it, and he wants Timothy to know it, because Timothy is going to need the encouragement.\n\nFifteen centuries later Thomas More sits in a stone room in the Tower of London with a piece of coal, because his ink has been taken away, and writes a letter to his daughter Margaret. He has been in the Tower for over a year. He has refused the Oath of Supremacy. He has been visited by his wife, who has scolded him for his obstinacy, and by his daughter, who has been more careful. He has been begged by everyone he loves to find some interpretation, some swearing-with-mental-reservation, some way out. He will be executed in July of 1535, six months from the letter.\n\nThe excerpt above is from a letter he wrote earlier, in 1534, when there was still some hope of release. \"Surely, Megge, a fainter heart than thy frail father hath, canst thou not have.\" He is telling her that he is afraid. He is not a hero in his own self-understanding. He is a frightened middle-aged lawyer who has discovered that his conscience will not let him swear what the king has commanded, and who is now waiting to see what it costs.\n\nThis is the right note to end the week on, because the whole tradition of Christian conscience finds its sharpest case in More, and More's own version of himself is the opposite of heroic. He calls himself frail. He admits the fainting heart. He tells his daughter not to worry about him while admitting in the same sentence that he looks up sadly. He has none of the bronze certainty we sometimes attribute to the canonized. He has only the witness inside, the one Paul wrote about in Romans 2, the one he has spent his life listening to, and now that witness has told him a thing he cannot unhear and he is going to die for it.\n\nA few features of the letter are worth holding.\n\nFirst, the affection. He calls her Megge. He notes that she has lost her worldly substance because of him. He worries about her more than about himself. The conscience that brought him to the Tower has not made him cold; it has, if anything, made him warmer. The caricature of the conscience-driven man as severe and distant is exactly wrong. The serious cases tend to go the other way. The thing that lets More refuse the oath is the same thing that makes him a tender father, and he has no need to choose between the two.\n\nSecond, the honesty about fear. \"A fainter heart than thy frail father hath, canst thou not have.\" He does not pretend to a courage he does not feel. He is not putting on a performance for Margaret or for posterity. He is telling the truth about the interior weather. The tradition does not require pretended courage. It requires only honest action. He is afraid and he is going to do the thing anyway, and he hopes God will stay him with his holy hand, because he knows he cannot stay himself.\n\nThird, the location of his confidence. It is not in himself. It is in God's mercy. He says he trusts that God will not suffer him to fall wretchedly from His favor. The confidence is borrowed. He has none of his own. This is the right way to hold a conscientious refusal — not as a confident assertion of one's own integrity but as a trembling hand on a robe one is not sure of holding.\n\nFourth, and this is the line that haunts every lawyer who reads it carefully: \"I have a good hope that God shall make me a strong champion in His own quarrel.\" Notice the grammar. Not that he will be a strong champion. That God will make him one. The strength is not in More. It is loaned, for the occasion, by the one whose quarrel it is. The lawyer's vocabulary should retain this. When the moment comes — the markup you cannot accept, the certification you cannot sign, the assignment you have to step off — the strength is not yours to produce on demand. You ask for it. You receive it or you do not. You go anyway.\n\nMost lawyers will never have a Tower. The drama of refusal in a modern practice is small and bureaucratic. You write the email saying you cannot take the assignment. You schedule the meeting and say the thing once, in a sentence. You leave the firm. You stay at the firm and pay the smaller cost of being known as the one who would not do the thing. These are not scaffolds. But they are the same exercise on a smaller scale, and More's letter is for them too. The frail father with the fainting heart is the patron saint not of the great heroes but of the ordinary advocates who, on a Tuesday afternoon in March, decline to do a small wrong and pay a small price.\n\nRoper, More's son-in-law and biographer, writes that as More was led to his execution he tipped the executioner and joked about his beard. The famous lines — \"the king's good servant, but God's first\" — were spoken on the scaffold. They are the public version. The private version is the letter to Megge. The man who said the public sentence had spent twenty years in private letters and prayers becoming the kind of man who could.\n\nThe week closes with him. Not because you will be More — almost certainly you will not — but because you are being formed, every Tuesday, into the kind of advocate who, if the moment came, could write the letter. The witness inside you is the same witness More listened to. He is patient. He has time. He is not in a hurry. He is waiting to see what kind of lawyer you are becoming.\n\nBe of good cheer, my good daughter. Take no thought for me. I have a good hope that God shall make me a strong champion in His own quarrel.",
    prayer:
      "Lord, I am not Thomas More. I am a frail lawyer with a fainting heart, in a small ordinary practice, with small ordinary refusals to make. When the moment comes, do not let me trust in my own strength. Make me, by your mercy, a strong champion in your own quarrel. And let the people I love be able to read, in my small choices, the same hope that More gave to Margaret.",
